    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-98333",
      "knowledge_point_id": "27e3c802-d356-5a2f-bdda-218144956722",
      "knowledge_point": "Estimating the Value of a Two-Variable Function",
      "problem": "Let $f(x,y)$ be a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(3,2)=9,\\,{f}_{x}(x,y)=-\\frac{3x}{\\sqrt{26-{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}}},\\,{f}_{y}(x,y)=-\\frac{6y}{\\sqrt{26-{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(3,2),$ estimate the value of $f(3.05,2.05).$",
      "worked_solution": "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a,b)+∇f(a,b)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(3,2)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(3,2)+∇f(3,2)\\cdot ⟨x-3,y-2⟩ \\\\  & =f(3,2)+⟨{f}_{x}(3,2),{f}_{y}(3,2)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-3,y-2⟩ \\\\  & =9+⟨{f}_{x}(3,2),{f}_{y}(3,2)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-3,y-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find ${f}_{x}(3,2)$ and ${f}_{y}(3,2)$ using the given partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(3,2) & =-\\frac{3(3)}{\\sqrt{26-(3)^{2}-2(2)^{2}}}=-3 \\\\ {f}_{y}(3,2) & =-\\frac{6(2)}{\\sqrt{26-(3)^{2}-2(2)^{2}}}=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈9+⟨-3,-4⟩\\cdot ⟨x-3,y-2⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can estimate $f(3.05,2.05)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=3.05$ and $y=2.05,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3.05,2.05) & ≈9+⟨-3,-4⟩\\cdot ⟨3.05-3,2.05-2⟩ \\\\  & =9+⟨-3,-4⟩\\cdot ⟨0.05,0.05⟩ \\\\  & =9-0.15-0.2 \\\\  & =8.65\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
