    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-98328",
      "knowledge_point_id": "27e3c802-d356-5a2f-bdda-218144956722",
      "knowledge_point": "Estimating the Value of a Two-Variable Function",
      "problem": "Suppose that $f(x,y)$ is a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(-1,-2)=4,\\,{f}_{x}(-1,-2)=4,\\,{f}_{y}(-1,-2)=-2.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(-1,-2),$ estimate the value of $f(-1.05,-1.96).$",
      "worked_solution": "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a,b)+∇f(a,b)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(-1,-2)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(-1,-2)+∇f(-1,-2)\\cdot ⟨x+1,y+2⟩ \\\\  & =f(-1,-2)+⟨{f}_{x}(-1,-2),{f}_{y}(-1,-2)⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,y+2⟩ \\\\  & =4+⟨4,-2⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,y+2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(-1.05,-1.96)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=-1.05$ and $y=-1.96,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1.05,-1.96) & ≈4+⟨4,-2⟩\\cdot ⟨-1.05+1,-1.96+2⟩ \\\\  & =4+⟨4,-2⟩\\cdot ⟨-0.05,0.04⟩ \\\\  & =4-0.20-0.08 \\\\  & =3.72\\end{aligned}\n$$",
