    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-129636",
      "knowledge_point_id": "27e3c802-d356-5a2f-bdda-218144956722",
      "knowledge_point": "Estimating the Value of a Two-Variable Function",
      "problem": "Let $f(x,y)$ be a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(1,3)=4,\\,{f}_{x}(x,y)=2x+y,\\,{f}_{y}(x,y)=x.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,3),$ estimate the value of $f(1.04,3.03).$",
      "worked_solution": "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a,b)+∇f(a,b)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,3)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(1,3)+∇f(1,3)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-3⟩ \\\\  & ≈f(1,3)+⟨{f}_{x}(1,3),{f}_{y}(1,3)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-3⟩ \\\\  & ≈4+⟨{f}_{x}(1,3),{f}_{y}(1,3)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find ${f}_{x}(1,3)$ and ${f}_{y}(1,3)$ using the given partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,3) & =2(1)+3=5 \\\\ {f}_{y}(1,3) & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈4+⟨5,1⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-3⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can estimate $f(1.04,3.03)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=1.04$ and $y=3.03,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1.04,3.03) & ≈4+⟨5,1⟩\\cdot ⟨1.04-1,3.03-3⟩ \\\\  & =4+⟨5,1⟩\\cdot ⟨0.04,0.03⟩ \\\\  & =4+5(0.04)+1(0.03) \\\\  & =4+0.2+0.03 \\\\  & =4.23\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
