      "math_academy_id": "q-129631",
      "knowledge_point_id": "27e3c802-d356-5a2f-bdda-218144956722",
      "knowledge_point": "Estimating the Value of a Two-Variable Function",
      "problem": "Suppose that $f(x,y)$ is a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(2,0)=0,\\,{f}_{x}(2,0)=0,\\,{f}_{y}(2,0)=2.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(2,0),$ estimate the value of $f(2.01,0.02).$",
      "worked_solution": "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a,b)+∇f(a,b)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(2,0)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(2,0)+∇f(2,0)\\cdot ⟨x-2,y-0⟩ \\\\  & =f(2,0)+⟨{f}_{x}(2,0),{f}_{y}(2,0)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-2,y⟩ \\\\  & =0+⟨0,2⟩\\cdot ⟨x-2,y⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,2⟩\\cdot ⟨x-2,y⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(2.01,0.02)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=2.01$ and $y=0.02,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2.01,0.02) & ≈⟨0,2⟩\\cdot ⟨2.01-2,0.02⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,2⟩\\cdot ⟨0.01,0.02⟩ \\\\  & =0(0.01)+2(0.02) \\\\  & =0+0.04 \\\\  & =0.04\\end{aligned}\n$$",
