    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-98326",
      "knowledge_point_id": "27e3c802-d356-5a2f-bdda-218144956722",
      "knowledge_point": "Estimating the Value of a Two-Variable Function",
      "problem": "Suppose that $f(x,y)$ is a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(2,1)=2,\\,{f}_{x}(2,1)=-1,\\,{f}_{y}(2,1)=-1.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(2,1),$ estimate the value of $f(1.98,0.94).$",
      "worked_solution": "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a,b)+∇f(a,b)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(2,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(2,1)+∇f(2,1)\\cdot ⟨x-2,y-1⟩ \\\\  & =f(2,1)+⟨{f}_{x}(2,1),{f}_{y}(2,1)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-2,y-1⟩ \\\\  & =2+⟨-1,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨x-2,y-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(1.98,0.94)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=1.98$ and $y=0.94,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1.98,0.94) & ≈2+⟨-1,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨1.98-2,0.94-1⟩ \\\\  & =2+⟨-1,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨-0.02,-0.06⟩ \\\\  & =2+(-1)(-0.02)+(-1)(-0.06) \\\\  & =2+0.02+0.06 \\\\  & =2.08\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
