    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-98340",
      "knowledge_point_id": "b1639105-dca4-50d1-8d86-a7aed03ba0b5",
      "knowledge_point": "Estimating the Value of a Three-Variable Function",
      "problem": "Suppose that $f(x,y,z)$ is a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(1,-1,2)=6,\\,{f}_{x}(1,-1,2)=-4,\\,{f}_{y}(1,-1,2)=-1,\\,{f}_{z}(1,-1,2)=3.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,-1,2)$ estimate the value of $f(1.04,-0.99,2.02).$",
      "worked_solution": "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈f(a,b,c)+∇f(a,b,c)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b,z-c⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,-1,2)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(1,-1,2)+∇f(1,-1,2)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y+1,z-2⟩ \\\\  & =f(1,-1,2)+⟨{f}_{x}(1,-1,2),{f}_{y}(1,-1,2),{f}_{z}(1,-1,2)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y+1,z-2⟩ \\\\  & =6+⟨-4,-1,3⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y+1,z-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(1.04,-0.99,2.02)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=1.04,$ $y=-0.99,$ and $z=2.02$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1.04,-0.99,2.02) & ≈6+⟨-4,-1,3⟩\\cdot ⟨1.04-1,-0.99+1,2.02-2⟩ \\\\  & =6+⟨-4,-1,3⟩\\cdot ⟨0.04,0.01,0.02⟩ \\\\  & =6-0.16-0.01+0.06 \\\\  & =5.89\\end{aligned}\n$$",
