    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-98348",
      "knowledge_point_id": "b1639105-dca4-50d1-8d86-a7aed03ba0b5",
      "knowledge_point": "Estimating the Value of a Three-Variable Function",
      "problem": "Let $f(x,y,z)$ be a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,2,-3)=-6, & \\,{f}_{x}(x,y,z)=yz,\\,{f}_{y}(x,y,z)=xz,\\,{f}_{z}(x,y,z)=xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,2,-3),$ estimate the value of $f(1.01,1.95,-3.02).$",
      "worked_solution": "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈f(a,b,c)+∇f(a,b,c)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b,z-c⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,2,-3)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(1,2,-3)+∇f(1,2,-3)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-2,z+3⟩ \\\\  & =-6+⟨{f}_{x}(1,2,-3),{f}_{y}(1,2,-3),{f}_{z}(1,2,-3)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-2,z+3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find ${f}_{x}(1,2,-3)$, ${f}_{y}(1,2,-3)$ and ${f}_{z}(1,2,-3),$ using the given partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,2,-3) & =2(-3)=-6 \\\\ {f}_{y}(1,2,-3) & =1(-3)=-3 \\\\ {f}_{z}(1,2,-3) & =1(2)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈-6+⟨-6,-3,2⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-2,z+3⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can estimate $f(1.01,1.95,-3.02)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=1.01,y=1.95$ and $z=-3.02,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1.01,1.95,-3.02) & ≈-6+⟨-6,-3,2⟩\\cdot ⟨1.01-1,1.95-2,-3.02+3⟩ \\\\  & =-6+⟨-6,-3,2⟩\\cdot ⟨0.01,-0.05,-0.02⟩ \\\\  & =-6-0.06+0.15-0.04 \\\\  & =-5.95\\end{aligned}\n$$",
