      "math_academy_id": "q-129659",
      "knowledge_point_id": "b1639105-dca4-50d1-8d86-a7aed03ba0b5",
      "knowledge_point": "Estimating the Value of a Three-Variable Function",
      "problem": "Suppose that $f(x,y,z)$ is a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(3,2,1)=12,\\,{f}_{x}(3,2,1)=4,\\,{f}_{y}(3,2,1)=12,\\,{f}_{z}(3,2,1)=36.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(3,2,1)$ estimate the value of $f(2.97,2.1,1.04).$",
      "worked_solution": "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈f(a,b,c)+∇f(a,b,c)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b,z-c⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(3,2,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(3,2,1)+∇f(3,2,1)\\cdot ⟨x-3,y-2,z-1⟩ \\\\  & =12+⟨{f}_{x}(3,2,1),{f}_{y}(3,2,1),{f}_{z}(3,2,1)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-3,y-2,z-1⟩ \\\\  & =12+⟨4,12,36⟩\\cdot ⟨x-3,y-2,z-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(2.97,2.1,1.04)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=2.97,$ $y=2.1,$ and $z=1.04$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2.97,2.1,1.04) & ≈12+⟨4,12,36⟩\\cdot ⟨2.97-3,2.1-2,1.04-1⟩ \\\\  & =12+⟨4,12,36⟩\\cdot ⟨-0.03,0.1,0.04⟩ \\\\  & =12+4(-0.03)+12(0.1)+36(0.04) \\\\  & =12-0.12+1.2+1.44 \\\\  & =14.52\\end{aligned}\n$$",
