    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-129745",
      "knowledge_point_id": "0015e19e-222b-5c5b-a020-93392c50c10e",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Best Linear Approximation of a Multivariable Function",
      "problem": "Let $f(x,y)=2{x}^{2}-y.$ Find the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(0,-1).$",
      "worked_solution": "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a,b)+∇f(a,b)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(0,-1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(0,-1)+∇f(0,-1)\\cdot ⟨x,y+1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the definition of $f,$ we have that $f(0,-1)=1.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x} & =4x\\, &  & ⇒\\, & {f}_{x}(0,-1)=0, \\\\ {f}_{y} & =-1 &  & ⇒\\, & {f}_{y}(0,-1)=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient of the function at the point $(0,-1)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(0,-1) & =⟨0,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the linear approximation of the function $f$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(0,-1)+∇f(0,-1)\\cdot ⟨x,y+1⟩ \\\\  & ≈1+⟨0,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨x,y+1⟩ \\\\  & ≈1+0-(y+1) \\\\  & ≈-y.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
